#metadata(title = "PDF Columns Review", author = "Mote")
#document(
  default_font = "body",
  default_size = pt(11),
  page = { size = "letter", orientation = "portrait" },
  margin = { top = pt(54), right = pt(60), bottom = pt(54), left = pt(60), header = pt(24), footer = pt(28) }
)
#font(
  name = "body",
  latin = "Verdana",
  files = {
    regular = "/System/Library/Fonts/Supplemental/Verdana.ttf",
    bold = "/System/Library/Fonts/Supplemental/Verdana Bold.ttf",
    italic = "/System/Library/Fonts/Supplemental/Verdana Italic.ttf",
    bold_italic = "/System/Library/Fonts/Supplemental/Verdana Bold Italic.ttf"
  }
)

#header(distance = pt(18), height = pt(18))[Mote Columns Review #tab() Page #page_number()]
#footer(distance = pt(18))[Generated comparison fixture]

# PDF And DOCX Column Flow

This opening content is ordinary single-column markdown. It confirms the document can start naturally and only opt into columns for the part that needs them.

#section(
  properties = { start = "next_page", vertical_alignment = "top" },
  columns = { count = 2, gap = pt(30), separator = true },
  page_numbering = { restart = true, start = 1 }
)[
  ## Left Column

  Mote sections can carry page-level layout without forcing the entire document to become verbose. This section contains enough text to exercise wrapping inside the current column.

  The parser should accept ordinary paragraphs, nested inline marks, and list syntax inside the section body. The emitter should keep these as editable Word paragraphs and readable PDF text.

  - Plain markdown remains the starting point.
  - Directives add layout only where the author needs them.
    - Nested list items keep their hierarchy.
    - Inline `code`, **strong text**, and _emphasis_ stay inside the item.
  - A column break can move following content to the next column.

  #break(type = "column")

  ## Right Column

  The second column starts after an explicit column break. This is useful for policies, brochures, contract terms, and other documents where columns are a local section-level choice.

  Keep this text readable. Keep the section properties local. Keep the source simple enough that an LLM can generate it without needing hidden schema details.

  > A blockquote inside a two-column section should still behave like normal content.
]

#section(properties = { start = "next_page" }, columns = { count = 1 })[
  # Back To One Column

  This final section returns to one column. That is the progressive path we want: markdown first, then layout directives only where they carry useful intent.
]
